{
  "concept": "A decoder-only transformer predicts the next token at every position in parallel, using causally masked self-attention so each position sees only itself and earlier tokens, then maps each output vector to a vocabulary distribution via linear + softmax.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left-to-right pipeline layout with four column headings: 'Word embeddings', 'Transformer with masked attention', 'Linear + softmax', 'Probability of target token'",
    "Input token labels stacked vertically on the far left with arrows: <start>, It, takes, great, courage, to, let",
    "Seven word-embedding vectors drawn as horizontal rows of empty white cells (about 6 cells each), enclosed in a dashed rounded rectangle",
    "Bus/bracket connector gathering the seven embedding rows into a single thick arrow entering the transformer block",
    "Large grey rounded rectangle with dashed border = one decoder layer, containing all internal sublayers",
    "Orange/terracotta tall rectangle = masked self-attention block, with internal lower-triangular fan of thin lines showing each position attending only to itself and earlier positions",
    "Residual bypass wire routed over the attention block into a circled plus (⊕) addition node",
    "Teal vertical rounded bar = LayerNorm / intermediate feature vectors after attention",
    "Column of seven small blue-grey rounded capsules = position-wise feed-forward network applied to each token independently",
    "Second residual bypass wire over the feed-forward column into a second circled plus (⊕) node",
    "Second teal vertical rounded bar = LayerNorm / output feature vectors of the layer",
    "Annotation '(×K)' with ellipsis '…' between the transformer block and the Linear + softmax column indicating K repeated layers",
    "Column of seven teal rounded capsules = shared 'Linear + softmax' head, one per position, each fed by an arrow",
    "Seven output probability rows drawn as blue-grey cells with a dotted gap and ellipsis in the middle, representing a vocabulary-length distribution",
    "Vocabulary axis labels written vertically under the first output row: a, aardvark, abacus, …, zeta, zero",
    "Target token labels on the far right of each output row: It, takes, great, courage, to, let, yourself",
    "Arrows throughout connecting embeddings → transformer → linear/softmax → probability rows",
    "Tooltip/annotation panel area for click-to-inspect block descriptions"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "numLayers",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of decoder layers K",
      "range": [1, 6, 1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "That the '(×K)' notation is shorthand for stacking identical masked-attention + feed-forward layers; depth is repetition of the same block, not new machinery, and the sequence length and vector shape are unchanged at every layer."
    },
    {
      "id": "maskMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Attention pattern",
      "options": ["Causal (masked)", "Unmasked (bidirectional)"],
      "default": "Causal (masked)",
      "teaches": "Why the attention fan is lower-triangular: with the mask off, position 'to' could read the answer 'yourself' from a later position, so next-token prediction becomes trivial and the model learns nothing — the mask is what makes autoregressive training honest."
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectBlock",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "The specific job of each stage — embeddings turn tokens into vectors, masked attention mixes information across allowed positions, ⊕ residual adds preserve the original signal, Linear + softmax projects to vocabulary scores, and each probability row is a distribution over the whole vocabulary for one target token."
    },
    {
      "id": "predictTrace",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Trace prediction of 'yourself'",
      "states": ["idle", "prefix", "maskedAttention", "residualAdd", "linearSoftmax", "yourself"],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "How one output is produced end to end: the prefix <start>…let lights up, only that row's allowed attention lines fire, the residual adds carry the vector forward through the stack, and the softmax at the last position places probability mass on 'yourself'."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One layer, one pipeline",
      "narration": "Seven tokens enter as embedding vectors, pass through a single decoder layer, and each position leaves with its own output vector. Notice the sequence length never changes — one vector in, one vector out per position.",
      "state": { "numLayers": 1, "maskMode": "Causal (masked)", "predictTrace": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Every position predicts at once",
      "narration": "Each output vector goes through the same Linear + softmax head to give a probability distribution over the whole vocabulary. The targets are the inputs shifted by one: 'It' is predicted at the <start> position, 'yourself' at the 'let' position.",
      "state": { "numLayers": 1, "maskMode": "Causal (masked)", "predictTrace": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why the triangle",
      "narration": "Switch the mask off and the attention fan becomes full: each position can now see future tokens, so the 'let' position could simply copy 'yourself' from its neighbour. Switch back and only backward-looking connections remain.",
      "state": { "numLayers": 1, "maskMode": "Unmasked (bidirectional)", "predictTrace": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Trace one prediction",
      "narration": "With the causal mask restored, follow the prefix <start> It takes great courage to let through its allowed attention lines, the two residual additions, and the softmax that concentrates probability on 'yourself'.",
      "state": { "numLayers": 1, "maskMode": "Causal (masked)", "predictTrace": "yourself" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stack it K times",
      "narration": "Expanding ×K reveals identical layers chained together — same mask, same shapes, more mixing. Depth adds representational power without changing the input/output interface of the block.",
      "state": { "numLayers": 5, "maskMode": "Causal (masked)", "predictTrace": "idle" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The lower-triangular mask is the whole trick: it lets the decoder train on every next-token prediction in the sentence simultaneously, because no position is ever allowed to peek at the token it is being asked to predict.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide horizontal diagram, white background, four column headings in a serif/LaTeX-like font across the top. Far left: token words with short arrows into seven stacked row-vectors of empty white cells outlined in black, all wrapped in a dashed rounded rectangle, then gathered by a bracket into one thick arrow. Center: large grey-filled rounded rectangle with a dashed dark border. Inside it, left to right: a tall terracotta/orange rectangle crossed by many thin dark lines forming a lower-triangular fan (lines only go from a source row down/forward to equal-or-earlier target rows); a residual wire that leaves the input bus, arcs over the orange block, and drops into a circled plus; a slim teal vertical rounded bar; then a vertical column of seven small blue-grey rounded capsules each fed by its own arrow, a second residual arc into a second circled plus, and a second teal vertical bar. Just right of the block, the label '(×K)' above a horizontal ellipsis of three dots on the main arrow. Right of that: a column of seven teal rounded capsules (Linear + softmax), each arrowing into a probability row made of blue-grey squares — three cells, a dotted gap with '…', then two more cells — with target words It, takes, great, courage, to, let, yourself at the right end. Vertical vocabulary labels (a, aardvark, abacus, …, zeta, zero) beneath the bottom probability row. Highlighting: clicked block gets a bright outline and a small tooltip card; the traced path renders in a warm accent color with the non-participating attention lines faded; when the mask is toggled off, the fan fills into a complete bipartite pattern in a cautionary color with a short warning note ('future tokens visible — prediction is trivial'). Expanded K draws repeated grey layer rectangles slightly overlapped in depth-like offset, replacing the '(×K)' ellipsis."
}

